      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for these! The contrast is definitely starting to show. I want to push the \"satire\" element a bit further to make sure the readers immediately get the class divide. \n\nCan you rewrite the local resident's monologue? I want it to feel a bit more \"gritty\" and raw. Maybe use more street slang, talk about how the neighborhood is getting \"sketchy\" or \"dangerous\" because of the changes, and show a bit more of that frustrated, unpolished edge. I want the reader to feel",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "of the authentic feel of the neighborhood the new resident doesn’t get.\n\n\"Been here since the crib was still a faded buildings. Bradenton Avenue just spun rounds flyin' on the Rails, it's like it sorted real folks from dope cash mobs rolling in. Kept smellin' breakfast all around, seeing the old timers up and down the block with yarn got breakdancing. Now there are posers and next-door-banking hustlers screamin' 'Good morning, I’m Ben from the Reserve Bank'.\n\nWhen I did the early-morning rush to cash in on union checks, ain't no trace, just ghost fades past was it a night before the whole junkets came from the upstate. Now there're sketchy birds shockin' a slug roll juice, no one stepping out that dusk to welcome a fam member comin' down.\n\nHomies used to sleep easy homie, they jacked their home nothin' faster than a bubble destined to pop. Now I tryn' scatter some savvy backyard beamin, every late night. Got no choice, no cousin left to share the worry. Gentrification mad as hell, but I'll trade the quaintness for things to lay low and safe from those real estate",
